{
  "info": {
    "name": "Player Service",
    "description": "Player Service owns the moderator player accounts: identity, credentials, profile, friends, XP, level, shift history and the disciplinary log.\n\nExactly one endpoint is in the CPR communication contract - GET /api/v1/players/{player_id}. Everything else is an extension the root README pre-authorises (\"Account features (register, login, friends, profile editing) will be added when the client is designed\") and is labelled \"beyond contract\" in the folder names.\n\nThere is no authentication: the contract puts it out of scope for now and no token issuer exists in the system yet, so no request here carries a token. As partial mitigation no endpoint returns `email`.\n\nMOCKED BUSINESS LOGIC. Server Moderation Session Service does not exist yet and neither does the transport that would carry its events, so nothing publishes `session.ended`. The \"dev\" folder posts that exact envelope to POST /api/v1/dev/events/session-ended, which hands it to the same ApplySessionEnded a real client will call - so XP, shift history, the disciplinary log and the event deduplication the contract requires are all exercisable today. Nothing else in this service is mocked.\n\nPREREQUISITES. Run the service with its database (`./scripts/run.sh` in the player-service repository, or the team compose) on host port 8087. On an empty database it seeds six players; the {{dima_id}}, {{maxim_id}} and {{vlad_id}} variables are the ids the CPR README and the other services' collections already use. {{dima_id}} carries xp 1250 / 12 completed shifts, so the contract's published example response is reproducible verbatim.\n\nORDER. Folders are written to run top to bottom on a freshly seeded database. Requests whose names start with a number belong to a sequence and depend on the ones before them. The last folder is destructive; `make clean-data` in the service repository restores the seed.",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
